When carrying out air health transport, it is necessary to take into account a series of physical factors that may influence the pathology of the evacuated patient:
• Changes in atmospheric pressure;
• Changing temperature;
• Movement of the aircraft;
• Increased levels of carbon dioxide (CO2) and decreased oxygen tension (PO2);
• Alterations in barometric pressure.
All these variables can cause changes in the patient's condition during the transfer. The medical crew must therefore be able to recognize early signs of problems arising from these environmental conditions, so as to intervene promptly and effectively.
The low atmospheric pressures typical of altitude flight (from about 1,500 m above sea level upwards) can lead to a significant reduction in the partial pressure of oxygen, with consequent hypoxemia. To avoid this problem, medical crews on board usually carry oxygen therapy equipment, which allows the administration of high concentrations of oxygen to patients who need it.
Another important aspect is related to temperature changes. Aircraft cabins are generally refrigerated to create a slight overpressure inside and thus ensure better retention of the oxygen in the aircraft. However, such low temperatures can have adverse effects on certain patients. For example, hypothermia can occur in trauma patients if they are not adequately wrapped or clothed before being placed in the aircraft. Hypothermia can also affect patients suffering from shock or burns, as well as newborn infants. During long flights, it is advisable to monitor body temperature and prevent dangerous temperature drops.
Finally, it should be emphasized that the movements and vibrations typical of helicopter flights, especially when the helipad is unstable, can adversely affect the patient's clinical condition. This risk can be reduced by taking particular care when loading the patient onto the aircraft.
